Wire Flutter's scale recognizer (0015) into touch dispatch: two or more fingers on an `on_pinch` element pinch it.

What:
- `PinchEvent` gains `touch: Option<TouchPinch>`: `None` for a trackpad pinch, for a touch pinch
  `TouchPinch::Start` / `Update` / `End` carrying Flutter's `ScaleStartDetails` / `ScaleUpdateDetails` /
  `ScaleEndDetails` (0015's `gpui::scale`), and a crate-private owner read with `PinchEvent::touch_owner()`, so
  backends build trackpad pinches with `PinchEvent::new(position, delta, modifiers, phase)`. A touch pinch also
  fills the trackpad fields: `position` is the focal point, `delta` the scale's change since the last event,
  `phase` is `TouchPinch::phase()`, so code written for trackpad zoom zooms with fingers too.
- `gestures/pinch.rs`: `PinchArena`, Flutter's gesture arena between one element's `ScaleGestureRecognizer` and
  the window's touch recognizer, as an explicit state (`Idle`, `Single`, `Contested`, `Won`), fed the touch
  recognizer's claim (`TouchClaim`: none, possible, pan, press). One finger stays the touch recognizer's (its hit
  slop is below the scale's pan slop); a second finger on the same element hands both to the scale recognizer;
  when the scale passes its slop (`gpui::slops` from the window's `GestureTuning::touch_slop`) the touch
  recognizer gets `Cancelled` for them and they reach only the pinch until they lift. When the touch recognizer
  wins first, as Flutter's drag and long press accept in every arena they share with the scale, the scale loses
  the touches it held: the one touch's pan before a second lands (a running pan never becomes a pinch, and a
  finger joining it is the pan's), the touches' focal point travelling past the hit slop in a contest (Flutter's
  drag adds up every pointer's movement: fingers dragged together pan, fingers spread apart pinch), a claimed long
  press or touch drag (two fingers held past the long-press deadline never pinch). A finger landing during a long
  press is the scale's alone (Flutter's one-pointer scale). A finger joining or
  leaving ends and restarts the gesture, as in Flutter; the last finger keeps it.
- Claims decide which element pinches: `TouchRoute::Gestures` carries every pinch element a touch joined, deepest
  first. `Window::join_pinch(touch, owner)` (public, for custom elements) joins it unless a deeper element claimed
  the touch (`on_touch`); a deeper element that only left it to gestures (a button) keeps its taps. The deepest
  pinches, unless an earlier finger is pinching an ancestor already (a finger on each of two nested pinch
  elements pinches the outer one, as every scale recognizer on a hit path holds the pointer in Flutter). The
  window dispatches the events with a synthesized touch `pointer_source`; the mouse stays where it was.
- div: `on_pinch` on an element with an id joins the touches that start over it (and leaves them to gestures, so an
  ancestor's `on_touch` cannot take them); its listeners get the touch pinch wherever the fingers move, only on that
  element, with `local_focal_point` relative to its bounds (`TouchPinch::localize`). Trackpad pinches keep
  upstream's hover rule.
- `scale.rs`: Flutter's `localFocalPoint` restored as `local_focal_point` on the start and update details (the
  recognizer sets the focal point; the receiving element makes it local, standing in for Flutter's transform).

Why: GPUI recognizes no pinch from touches (gestures.rs: "Pinch recognition is not implemented yet") and ignores
every finger after the first; touch platforms need pinch-to-zoom. The recognizer is Flutter's (0015); this is only
the glue, so it lives where the touch recognizer and element dispatch are. Reusing upstream's `PinchEvent` and
`on_pinch` keeps one listener for trackpad and touch pinches instead of a parallel API.

Depends on: 0004 (touch routes and claims), 0013 (`gpui::slops`, `play_trace`), 0014 (the tuning), 0015 (the
scale recognizer).

Test: `gestures::pinch::tests::touch_pinches_follow_flutters_arena` (pinch_tests.rs), traces through the test
platform (`play_trace`) into nested `on_pinch` divs: pinch out, pinch in on the nested element (deepest wins, local
focal point), rotate, a third finger joining, a finger lifted mid-pinch, a running pan that never becomes a pinch,
two fingers dragged together (pan), fingers spread apart after the first passed the hit slop (pinch), two fingers held past the long-press deadline (long press, no pinch), a finger
landing during a long press (one-finger pinch), a finger on each of two nested pinch elements (outer pinches), a
two-finger tap, one finger left before the slop, two fingers on an element without `on_pinch` (unchanged).

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

